course: adult-life
lesson: 43
title: Our street: when a guess becomes a reputation
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A reputation is what people think you are like. On a street it is often built from tiny things: one bad week with the bins, one shout heard through a window, one walk to school with a friend. Repeat a guess enough times and it sticks to a person like a label.
explain: Many puzzling things have a boring reason. A man who comes home at strange hours may be a hospital porter on night shifts. A woman who is never seen outside may use a wheelchair on a path with steps. Sunglasses in the rain may be for sensitive eyes.
explain: Scene: in Toronto, Grandad mutters about «the kids from the flats, always hanging round the shop, up to no good». What he can see is teenagers chatting after school. «Up to no good» is a judgement on a whole group from one picture, and that is how prejudice starts.
explain: Some gossip is lovely: Mrs Lee bought the park kids ice lollies with her bingo money; the Evanses invite the whole street to their fortieth anniversary. Good-news gossip that thanks someone or shares their own news is the best kind.
explain: And when you are truly worried about a child's safety, that is not gossip: tell a trusted adult, who knows who to talk to.
fact: A reputation can grow from one small moment repeated as «always»; one bad week is not a whole reputation.
fact: Strange hours often mean a shift worker, like a nurse, porter or baker, keeping everyone safe and fed.
fact: Before laughing at something odd, think of one boring reason for it.
fact: Judging a whole group, like «the kids from the flats», from one picture is how prejudice starts.
fact: When something bad happens to someone, like a burglary, blame the person who did it, not the person it happened to.
fact: Never comment on someone's weight; you don't know if they are ill, grieving or just healthier.
fact: One word can change a story completely: a wall that «fell down» is not a wall that was «taken down».
fact: Teasing people about who they like can stop them being friends at all.
fact: If you are really worried about a child being unsafe, tell a trusted adult instead of turning it into gossip.
fact: Not everything people do is about you; a doorbell camera is a common gadget, not an insult.
fact: Behind a funny line about someone there may be a sad story, like old pets that died.
fact: Good news can be gossip too, and the best stories are the ones people share themselves.
fact: One complaint is not a pattern; get facts before you warn people off a business.
fact: Some gossip can turn into something kind: a card, a collection, a thank you.
exercise: Mum: «Lollipop lady retiring after thirty years and nobody's organised a collection. Shameful.» What's really going on? | The school already has a card going round | Nobody at all has noticed she's leaving | She asked people not to give her anything | The school forgot she was retiring
exercise: Grandad: «Ted at 31's got a girlfriend half his age! At his age!» What's actually been seen? | Ted walking with a younger woman | Ted holding hands at his wedding | Ted telling people about his girlfriend | Ted buying her a ring in town
exercise: Big sister: «Number 12's teenager got suspended for a nose ring!» What can you actually see? | The new nose stud | The letter from school | The teenager crying at home | The head teacher at the door
exercise: Nan: «The Hartleys' extension FELL DOWN! Cowboy builders!» What really happened? | Builders took a wall down to fix a mistake | The whole extension fell down in the middle of the night | The builders ran off with the money | A storm blew the extension over
exercise: Auntie Bev: «Mr Novak's never home, comes back at strange hours. What does he even DO?» What does he do? | He's a hospital porter on shifts | He's up to something secret at night | He has no job and goes out all night | He has a second family across town
exercise: Dad: «The Robinsons are in that hot tub EVERY night with wine!» How often has Dad heard them? | Twice | Every single night | Every weekend all year | Only on bank holidays
exercise: Kids: «Corner house is empty cos someone DIED in it and no one will buy it!» What's the boring truth? | The old owner went into a care home and it's waiting for paperwork | A ghost scares off everyone who comes to look at it, so nobody stays | The house is falling down inside | The council has banned anyone living there
exercise: Mum: «Sharon's lost loads of weight, she must be ill.» What's the right thing to do? | Say nothing about her weight at all | Ask her straight out if she is ill | Tell others she might be ill | Ask her what diet she has been on
exercise: Grandad: «The kids from the flats are up to no good, that lot.» What's he doing? | Judging a whole group from one picture | Reporting a crime he has seen with his own eyes | Telling the truth about the flats | Warning the shop about thieves
exercise: Auntie Jen: «Thompsons' boy is going to be on the telly, a cooking show, he'll be famous!» What's really happened? | He applied and got an interview | He's already filmed the whole show | He's won the cooking show | He's been given his own show
exercise: Nan: «Somebody's feeding the foxes at 20, that's why they're on our bins.» What's the fuller picture? | 20 feeds hedgehogs, and foxes go to all bins | 20 puts out food every night just for the foxes | Foxes only come to streets with feeders | 20 keeps foxes as pets in the garden
exercise: Mrs Okafor: «Man at 6 shouts at his kids, whole street must've heard!» What is the honest fact? | A shout was heard from Number 6 one evening | He shouts at his children every single day | The whole street heard him every night | The police have been to Number 6 about it
exercise: Big brother: «Mr Price got a ticket outside his OWN house, the warden was laughing!» What really happened? | He parked on the zigzag lines | The warden gave a ticket for no reason | He was parked on his own drive | The warden is known to hate him
exercise: Auntie Bev: «Fiona's twins are always in trouble. Her ex never sees them, that's why.» What's known? | They were kept in at playtime last week | They are in trouble with their teacher every single day | Their dad never visits them at all | The school has told Fiona to move
exercise: Mum: «The Chens put up a camera. Must think we're all thieves.» What's a fairer thought? | It's a common gadget, maybe after a parcel theft | They are right, the street is full of thieves | They want to spy on the neighbours | It's rude and should be taken down
exercise: Nan: «Bungalow man's wife never comes out. Does she even exist?» What's the truth? | She uses a wheelchair and the path has steps | She doesn't exist, he lives alone | She's being kept inside by her husband against her will | She's a famous person hiding from fans
exercise: Grandad: «Dennis bought his third dog this year. Collects 'em like stamps.» What's behind it? | His two old dogs died, this one's a rescue | He gets bored of each dog after a month or so | He sells the dogs he doesn't want | He's breeding dogs to make money
test: Auntie Jen: «That woman from the big house wears sunglasses in the RAIN. Thinks she's a film star lol.» Which reason is the kind first guess? | Her eyes might be sensitive to light | She is trying to hide who she really is | She thinks she is better than everyone | She forgot to take them off from summer
test: Big sister: «Callum and Priya walked to school together, they're DEFINITELY going out!!» What might happen if everyone teases them? | They stop walking together, even as friends | They get married when they grow up | Nothing, teasing never changes anything at all between friends | They thank everyone for noticing
test: Mum: «Evans' fortieth anniversary party, the whole street's invited!» Is this gossip true? | Yes, they sent invitations in writing | No, it's just a rumour going round | Maybe, nobody has asked the Evanses | No, they've been married for twenty years
test: Dad: «Garage bloke's overcharging everyone, I bet. My mate paid double.» What's he really got? | One mate who thought his bill was high | Proof from lots and lots of unhappy customers | A list of the garage's old prices | A police report about the garage
test: Kids: «Mrs Lee won a hundred quid on the bingo and bought everyone ice cream!» It was twenty pounds and ice lollies. What kind of gossip is it? | Lovely gossip that thanks someone | Nasty gossip that hurts Mrs Lee | Dangerous gossip about her money | Gossip that should be reported
test: Dad: «Number 27 got burgled, back door wide open they say, some people never learn.» What's wrong with the last bit? | It blames the victims, not the burglar | It's fair, they should lock their doors | It's true, they were burgled twice | Nothing, the burglar was a neighbour
test: Your friend is scared: «Next door shouts every night and the kid cries a lot.» What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult about your worry | Tell everyone in class what you heard | Shout back at the neighbours yourself | Say nothing, it's none of your business
test: Grandad: «Mr Novak? Probably a burglar, out all night.» Then Mr Novak waves from the bus in a hospital uniform. What does it show? | Odd hours often mean a night-shift job | Mr Novak is in disguise as a porter | Grandad was right to be suspicious | Hospital workers never sleep at all
test: Nan: «Corner house, someone died there, nobody'll buy it now.» Who could this story really harm? | The family trying to sell it | The ghost living in the house | The children who tell the story | Nobody, it's just a spooky tale
test: Auntie Jen: «Thompson boy's gonna be famous, I've told everyone!» He doesn't get picked. How might he feel? | Embarrassed, as everyone expected to see him | Happy that everyone knew about him | Glad that Auntie Jen had told the whole street in advance | Nothing, he never wanted to be picked
test: Mum: «Hartleys' builders are cowboys, the extension FELL DOWN!» Which one word made it a disaster? | «Fell» instead of «taken down» | «Builders» instead of «workmen» | «Extension» instead of «room» | «Hartleys» instead of «neighbours»
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the assertion that adolescents congregating outside a shop are «up to no good»? | It constitutes a judgement upon an entire group from a single observation | It constitutes a verified report of criminal conduct by those present | It constitutes an official finding of the local police authority | It constitutes a neutral description of the observed behaviour
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a resident who departs and returns at irregular hours ought not to be regarded with suspicion. | Irregular hours are commonly attributable to shift employment | Irregular hours are prohibited by the regulations of the council | Residents are obliged to publish their working hours to neighbours | Persons who work at night are invariably exempt from the law
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the burglary at Number 27. | Responsibility for the offence rests with the perpetrator | Responsibility for the offence rests with the occupants | The occupants were found to have left all doors open | The occupants were subsequently fined by the police
exam: It is given that a child has reason to believe that another child may be unsafe at home. Determine the most appropriate conduct. | To communicate the concern to a trusted adult | To disseminate the concern among classmates | To confront the household concerned in person | To refrain from any action whatsoever
